methods.explanation:
Both equations contain the same factor \(k\), so division will eliminate it. Since \(2^r>0\), the first equation gives \(k>0\), making this division valid. Dividing the second equation by the first gives
\[
5=\frac{15}{3}
=\frac{k4^r}{k2^r}
=\frac{2^{2r}}{2^r}
=2^r.
\]
By definition, \(\log_2 5\) is the exponent to which \(2\) must be raised to obtain \(5\). This value is feasible: with \(k=3/5\), the two original right-hand sides are \(3\) and \(15\). Therefore \(r=\log_2 5\), choice \(\mathrm{D}\).
